  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Legionella risk is managed under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974, the L8 Approved Code of Practice and the HSG274 technical guidance</li><li>A school with a water system needs a legionella risk assessment and, where risk is present, a written scheme of routine checks: temperatures, flushing and asset maintenance</li><li>The summer holiday is a peak risk period for schools, because outlets sit unused and water stagnates</li><li>The log does not control legionella; the written scheme, carried out and acted on, does</li><li>Paying only for who actually works lets a trust log water checks across every school, with unlimited users on one dashboard</li></ul></div><h2>What the water monitoring log is for</h2><p>Legionella bacteria grow in water systems held at the wrong temperature and left to stagnate. The control regime is set out in the Health and Safety Executive's Approved Code of Practice L8 and the technical guidance HSG274, under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974. A school assesses its risk, and where risk is present, follows a written scheme: monthly sentinel temperatures, weekly flushing of little-used outlets, and periodic checks of tanks, thermostatic mixing valves and showerheads.</p><p>The log does not control legionella. The written scheme, carried out and acted on, does. What the log does is prove the routine happened, on time, at the right outlets, and flag when a reading falls outside the safe range so someone acts. This is general guidance, not legal advice; check the current L8 ACOP and HSG274 and your own legionella risk assessment.</p><h2>What a school water log records</h2><h3>1. Temperatures</h3><p>Monthly sentinel outlet temperatures, plus hot water flow and return, to confirm the system runs hot enough at the hot outlets and cold enough at the cold ones to control risk.</p><h3>2. Flushing</h3><p>Weekly flushing of infrequently used taps and showers, a common issue in schools where outlets in remote rooms sit unused, especially after the holidays.</p><h3>3. Asset checks</h3><p>Periodic checks and maintenance of cold water storage tanks, thermostatic mixing valves and showerheads, cleaned and descaled on the schedule the written scheme sets.</p><h2>Who holds the duty</h2><p>The duty to manage legionella risk sits with whoever controls the premises, which for a school is the responsible body: the local authority or governing body for a maintained school, the trust for an academy. The duty holder must appoint a competent person to help manage the risk, ensure a risk assessment is in place, and keep records of the routine monitoring. The DfE's Good Estate Management for Schools expects water safety to be managed and evidenced like any other statutory duty.</p><h2>Why schools are a special case</h2><p>A school half-empties for the summer, and again at Christmas and Easter, and during those weeks outlets in unused classrooms, changing rooms and remote toilets sit stagnant. Stagnation plus the wrong temperature is exactly the condition legionella needs. The first days back are when the scheme's flushing and temperature checks matter most. A written scheme that schedules flushing through and immediately after the holidays is how a school manages this rather than hopes.</p><h2>What to look for in a water monitoring tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site capture:</strong> the reading logged at the outlet, not back-filled before an audit</li><li><strong>Out-of-range flags:</strong> a hot outlet reading too cool, or a cold outlet too warm, should stand out and become an action</li><li><strong>Scheduled prompts:</strong> the weekly flush and monthly temperature check prompted when due, including around the holidays</li><li><strong>Trust-wide visibility:</strong> a central dashboard across every site, so the responsible body can see overdue checks</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, with a free tier of 20 reports a month, so every caretaker and site is free to add</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the routine water monitoring and flags out-of-range readings. It is not the legionella risk assessment and it is not the competent person. The risk assessment and the design of the written scheme stay with a competent specialist. Quickler holds the record that the checks were done, which is the part that most often goes missing. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your school or trust.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Do schools have to monitor for legionella?</h3><p>Yes, where a legionella risk is identified. Legionella risk is managed under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974, the L8 Approved Code of Practice and HSG274. A school must have a legionella risk assessment, and where risk is present, follow a written scheme of routine checks: temperatures, flushing of little-used outlets, and asset maintenance. Check your own risk assessment for what applies to your site.</p><h3>How often should a school check water temperatures?</h3><p>A typical written scheme records monthly sentinel outlet temperatures and hot water flow and return, with weekly flushing of little-used outlets, but the exact frequency comes from the school's own legionella risk assessment. Schools should pay particular attention to flushing around and after the school holidays, when outlets sit unused. Follow the schedule your competent person and risk assessment set.</p><h3>Does Quickler replace the legionella risk assessment?</h3><p>No. Quickler captures the routine monitoring the written scheme requires and holds the temperature and flushing log. It is not the legionella risk assessment and it is not the competent person. The risk assessment and the design of the scheme stay with a competent specialist. Quickler holds the record that the routine checks were carried out and flags out-of-range readings.</p><h3>Can I log water temperature checks over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's water monitoring workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The staff member receives each outlet check in their existing WhatsApp chat, types the temperature reading or confirms the flush, and the dated log generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the school's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/school-premises-inspection-software-uk.html\">School premises inspection software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/school-health-safety-audit-uk.html\">School health and safety audit checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/playground-equipment-inspection-uk.html\">Playground equipment inspection</a></li></ul>",
